nd a lot of time on brain biases. How do we naturally bias our thinking? Therefore, how do we do critical thinking differently? And how do we elevate that in the way we live and work, and particularly in this situation? And last but not least, they make decisions, they that write the report. There's not a report given to them that's blank. And to hear the sorts of things you hear at the end of a process like this, I have run hundreds of these things, but at the end of it, you hear people say, you know, this has changed my life. I've had people sign up to these things for the money. I had one woman, I'll tell her story because it's beautiful, it was for an energy organization. This woman signed up because she needed to get her teeth done. So she signed up to get the money because you pay people a stipend, it's minimum wage to be in the room, which I think is fair enough. They give a fair amount of time. Oh my god, she was the best participant I've ever had. She struggled with every single concept. She did the homework, she turned up, she challenged everyone in the room, and they got a really good result. And she is an advocate for that organization to this day because it changed her life. And I kid you not, just about at every closing session I run for a deliberative process, I cry. I'm that emotional, because you see the shift in people. They are like, I can make a difference. I am powerful. I've worked with others. This is a great thing. We could do more of this. This is how it works. And they

