xactly what happens in juries. And so it doesn't separate the governed from the governing except in so far as is absolutely necessary. It doesn't disturb the essential equality of people while accepting that there is a division of cognitive labor in any complex situation. Somebody has to be on that jury, twelve people have to be on that jury, and they have to sit through twelve days or whatever it is, and then they deliberate on that. And it's completely absurd to have a hundred people doing it or everyone doing it, although that's kind of what we do at the same time for our own entertainment through the press. So these very simple ways of thinking about the structures that govern us have really influenced me very strongly because I do think that, I mean, Anthony let the expression drop just a minute ago. He talked about representative democracy as if that meant elections. No, it can, you can talk about representative democracy and everyone does, as if that presupposes elections, but it could presuppose the alternative way to represent the people, which is what happens in juries. Representation by sampling rather than representation by elections, with all of the profound differences that that involves to equality, to the framing of what's going on. If you set up a competition, you'll get a certain kind of discussion. And we can see it every day because not only is our political system um profoundly competitive, but the medium through which it travels to us, the media, has always been competitive and is now much more competitive than it was. And it's more or Less doing to our political system what happens to a fly when you spray morteen on it, which is that you dissolve its nervous system. Just dead. So that's a bunch of thoughts. I'll just say one other thing, which is that Nicole spoke, well, about the tears in her eyes, I know what she means. She spoke about the fact that you can't run one of these exercises well, which Nicole does, without producing the kind of transformation in the people that she mentioned. And the way I put that is that we evolved on the African savannah to do that. And when people discover it, it completely astonishes them. And I wouldn't be going on about democracy so much if I just thought this was a terribly hard problem. It's not that hard. Because this other way to represent the people just sits there, and every opportunity it's given performs in an astonishing way, if you get it all close to it. But we don't, so it would be with the there is more than enough philanthropic money around for us to set up a standing citizen assembly of a hundred odd people that simply shadowed the federal parliament. No, don't ask for any permission. Just run it, turn people over every six months or two years or whatever you decide. I've got a few ideas about how you ultimately create a situation where it could become self-governing in the way that the House of Representatives or the Senate are, these are self-sovereign bodies that make their own decisions and generate their own tradition and go through their own history and their own crises and have their old hands who can help the newcomers and so on. So, anyway, that's what I think. I think we could I think, I mean, there are so many problems of human life where you come up with a really hard problem andor a really ingrained problem, and you basically don't really know how to solve it. You've got a few clues and you try some things out, but this, I don't think, I can't prove this to you, but I don't think that's one of these problems. I think the answer is staring us in the face. And it would be like passing through a portal. Um so that's what we can do, but nobody will. I can't do it because I don't have $10 million. Um and the people who do have all that money run little display shows here and there, and they go and talk to politicians, and the politicians say how good it is of them to do this stuff, and they say, you know, I had this great meeting with senior po